[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/4p3/2B1P3/2NP4/PPP2PPP/R1BQK1NR_b_KQkq_-_0_4"]

{ The Vienna set-up from the first chapter, with his dark bishop still at home. It has two good squares: c5, which you know, and b4, where it pins your knight to your king. }
4... Bb4 { His bishop pins your knight on c3 to your king, and that knight is the one guarding e4. How do you back it up without blocking your f pawn? [%cal Rb4e1,Gc3e4] } 5. Ne2 { The king knight comes to e2: it guards c3, so if he takes there a knight takes back, and the f pawn stays free. [%cal Ge2c3,Gf2f4] } (5. Nf3 { The natural knight move is a good one, but it sits in front of the f pawn and the Vienna storm is off. } 5... d5 6. exd5 Nxd5 { A normal, level game, just not the Vienna you came to learn. [%csl Rf3] }) (5. Bg5 { Pinning his knight right back is the most popular answer after the knight move. } 5... h6 6. Bxf6 Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 Qxf6 { Both bishops are gone and your c pawns are doubled. Level, but his queen is the livelier piece. [%csl Rc3] }) (5. Bd2 { Breaking the pin with the bishop is sound and quiet. } 5... O-O 6. Nge2 Bc5 { His bishop drops back to c5 and you reach the usual set-up a move slower. Level. }) (5. a3 { Asking the bishop at once only hands him the trade. } 5... Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 d5 { He doubles your pawns and strikes in the centre at once. Slightly better for him. [%csl Rc3] }) (5. f4 { The Vienna push, one move early: with your knight pinned, e4 is barely guarded. } 5... d5 6. exd5 Nxd5 7. Ne2 Bg4 { His knight and bishop pour in while your king is stuck in the middle. He is clearly better. [%csl Re1] }) 5... d5 { He hits back in the centre at once: his pawn on d5 attacks your bishop, and your knight on c3 is still pinned. [%cal Rd5c4] } 6. exd5 { Take the pawn: it was the one attacking your bishop, and after his knight takes back you can castle out of the pin. [%csl Gd5] } (6. Bb3 { Stepping back looks safe, and it walks into a pawn fork. } 6... d4 7. a3 Ba5 { His pawn on d4 hits your knight, and the pin keeps it rooted to c3. You lose a piece. [%cal Rd4c3,Ra5e1] }) (6. Bxd5 { Giving the bishop for the knight is playable, but it hands him the bishop pair. } 6... Nxd5 7. exd5 Qxd5 { His queen takes back in the centre and he keeps both bishops. Slightly better for him. }) (6. Bb5 { The bishop dodges to b5, but now nothing helps your pawn on e4. } 6... dxe4 7. dxe4 Nxe4 { He wins your pawn on e4 and comes out a pawn up. [%cal Rf6e4] }) (6. O-O { Castling out of the pin sounds right, but your bishop is still attacked. } 6... dxc4 7. dxc4 Be7 { Your bishop is gone for a single pawn. [%csl Rc4] }) 6... Nxd5 { His knight takes back, and from d5 it eyes your knight on c3. } 7. O-O { Castle: with your king off e1 the knight on c3 is no longer pinned, and your rook stands behind the f pawn. [%csl Gg1] } (7. a3 { The pawn to a3 puts the question to his bishop, and that is fine too. } 7... Bxc3+ 8. Nxc3 Nxc3 9. bxc3 { Everything comes off on c3 and your pawns are split, but the bishop pair balances it. Level. }) (7. Bd2 { Breaking the pin with the bishop is sound, just slower. } 7... Be6 8. O-O O-O { You castle a move later and reach the same kind of position. Level. }) (7. h3 { A useful little move, but the pin is still on. } 7... Be6 8. O-O Bc5 { You castle a move later, and his bishop has already found c5. Level. }) (7. Bxd5 { Trading your bishop for his knight gives him the bishop pair. } 7... Qxd5 8. O-O Qd7 { His queen is centralised and he keeps both bishops. Slightly better for him. }) 7... Be6 { He guards the knight on d5 with the bishop. Your f pawn wants to go to f4: what is stopping it? } 8. Bxd5 { Take the knight: it was the piece guarding f4. Your bishop gives itself up, and the f pawn is free. [%csl Gf4] } (8. Ne4 { The knight hop to e4 is popular and fine, just slower. } 8... Be7 9. d4 O-O { His knight stays on d5 and the f pawn push is off for now. Level. }) (8. Nxd5 { Trading knights leaves his bishop in charge of d5. } 8... Bxd5 9. a3 Be7 { Level, but his bishop on d5 now rules the centre. }) (8. Bb3 { Keeping the bishop leaves his knight on d5 where it is. } 8... Qd7 9. Nxd5 Bxd5 { The pieces come off on d5 anyway, on his terms. Level. }) (8. f4 { The f pawn at once, with his knight still on d5: count what is loose on c3 and c4. } 8... Nxc3 9. Nxc3 Qd4+ 10. Kh1 Bxc4 11. dxc4 Qxc4 { His queen check cleared the way, and he comes out a clean pawn up. [%csl Rc4] }) (8. Bd2 { Unpinning with the bishop is sound. } 8... Bc5 9. a3 Nxc3 10. Nxc3 { The knights come off on c3 and his bishops stay. Level. }) (8. Bb5 { Pinning his knight on c6 is playable too. } 8... O-O 9. a3 Be7 { He castles and backs the bishop off. Level. }) 8... Bxd5 { His bishop takes back on d5, and his knight is no longer there to guard f4. [%csl Gf4] } 9. f4 { The f pawn goes at last: it hits e5, your rook stands behind it on f1, and your king is already safe. That is the Vienna storm, with his pin long forgotten. [%cal Gf4e5] } (9. Bd2 { Unpinning first is fine, and the push can wait a move. } 9... Bc5 10. Kh1 f6 { He props e5 with his f pawn before you get going. Level. }) (9. a3 { Pushing a3 to question the bishop is also fine. } 9... Bxc3 10. Nxc3 O-O { The bishops come off and the game is level, with your push still to come. }) (9. Nxd5 { Taking the bishop brings his queen to the centre. } 9... Qxd5 10. a3 Be7 { His queen stands in the middle of the board and your push has lost its bite. Level. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the copycat bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
