[Role "exercise"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 3 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK1NR_b_KQkq_-_3_3"]

{ If he copies your Vienna bishop instead of bringing his king knight out, that copying carries a real price. }
3... Bc5 { He mirrors your bishop move, but his king knight is still asleep at home. What has he left unguarded? } 4. Qg4 { Out she comes, staring straight down the g column at his pawn on g7, and he has no comfortable answer. [%cal Gg4g7] } (4. Qh5 { The sister jump to h5 works just as well, hitting the same weak point. } 4... Qf6 5. Nf3 Nge7 { Two different roads to the same headache for him. Either queen move is fine. }) (4. Nf3 { Quiet, respectable development, with nothing wrong in it. } 4... Nf6 5. O-O O-O { That settles into a normal game, but the queen jump asks him far harder questions right now. }) (4. d3 { Solid and sound, and it keeps every one of your pieces defended. } 4... Nf6 5. Nf3 h6 { A pleasant, steady edge for you either way. The queen sortie is simply the sharper choice. }) (4. Nd5 { The knight jumps at the hole on d5, but nothing of yours is even attacking that square yet. } 4... Nf6 5. c3 O-O { He develops with a free tempo, and the jump has gained you nothing at all. }) 4... g6 { He blocks her with the pawn to g6, his most common answer, and it leaves holes on f6 and h6. Before you develop anything, look at his d pawn. His king to f8 is the other try, and it meets the same answer. [%csl Rf6,Rh6] } 5. Qf3 { She steps back to f3, out of reach of his d pawn, and threatens mate on f7 with your bishop's help. [%cal Gf3f7,Gc4f7] } (5. Qg3 { Just as good: from g3 she hits e5 and also stays out of the d pawn's way. } 5... d6 6. Nge2 f5 { A sharp game, and a good one for you either way. [%cal Gg3e5] }) (5. Qd1 { All the way home is safe, and it throws away the raid. } 5... Qh4 6. Qf3 f5 { His queen jumps out instead and he grabs the initiative. Roughly level. }) (5. Nf3 { A natural developing move, and it walks straight into his d pawn. } 5... d5 { The pawn hits your bishop, and behind it his bishop on c8 now stares at your queen. [%cal Rd5c4,Rc8g4] } 6. Qg3 dxc4 { The queen ran, so the bishop fell: a whole piece gone. [%csl Rc4] }) (5. d3 { Solid looking, but it does nothing about the d pawn either. } 5... d5 { Two targets at once: your bishop on c4, and your queen on the line his bishop has just opened. [%cal Rd5c4,Rc8g4] } 6. Qg3 dxc4 { Your queen had to run and the bishop was left behind. A piece down. }) (5. Nge2 { The right knight for the Vienna, at the wrong moment. } 5... d5 { One pawn move uncovers his bishop on your queen and hits your bishop on c4. [%cal Rd5c4,Rc8g4] } 6. Qf3 dxc4 { The queen stepped away and the bishop fell, for nothing at all. }) (5. Bb3 { Moving the bishop out of the d pawn's reach first is safe, but slow. } 5... d6 6. Qg3 Nf6 { Nothing is lost and nothing gained. Level. }) 5... Nf6 { His knight on f6 blocks the mate. Now develop, the Vienna way. Had his queen come to f6 instead, your knight to d5 would have hit her and c7. } 6. Nge2 { The king knight comes to e2, so the f pawn stays free and the queen keeps f3. [%csl Ge2][%cal Gf2f4] } (6. Nd5 { The knight jump is playable, but it simply gets traded. } 6... Nxd5 7. Bxd5 O-O { Knights off and his king is safe. Level. }) (6. d3 { Natural, and it lets his knight jump into d4 with a hit on your queen. } 6... Nd4 7. Qd1 c6 { Your queen is chased home and his knight rules d4. Slightly better for him. [%csl Rd4] }) (6. Bb3 { Tucking the bishop away lets his knight jump to d4 as well. } 6... Nd4 7. Qd1 d5 { His knight chases your queen home and his pawn strikes in the centre. He is better. }) (6. Qg3 { Stepping back to g3 invites his knight to h5. } 6... Nh5 7. Qf3 f5 { His knight kicks your queen and his f pawn strikes. Slightly better for him. }) 6... d6 { He props up e5 with the pawn. Castling would have met the same plan, and after h6 the knight jump to d5 is stronger still. Finish the set-up with one more pawn move. } 7. d3 { The pawn to d3 guards e4 and opens your dark bishop. The Vienna set-up is in place, with your queen on f3 aiming at f7 and his pawn on g6 leaving f6 and h6 weak. [%csl Gd3][%cal Gd3e4,Gf3f7] } (7. h3 { Just as good: it keeps his bishop off g4 before you push d3. } 7... Qe7 8. O-O Nd4 9. Nxd4 Bxd4 { The knights come off on d4 and you keep a pleasant edge. }) (7. Nd5 { The knight jump only trades itself off. } 7... Nxd5 8. exd5 Nd4 9. Nxd4 Bxd4 { The knights disappear and your edge with them. Level. }) (7. O-O { Castling is safe, but it lets his bishop pin on g4. } 7... Bg4 8. Qg3 h5 { His bishop and h pawn go after your queen, and your edge is gone. Level. }) (7. Qg3 { The queen to g3 walks into a hunt for your bishop. } 7... Na5 8. Bb3 c6 { His knight chases your bishop and he gets the level game he wanted. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the copycat queen raid"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/Cz1SyhDs"]
[Role "exercise"]
[FEN "r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p3/2B1P1Q1/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1B1K1NR b KQkq - 5 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p3/2B1P1Q1/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1B1K1NR_b_KQkq_-_5_4"]

{ The same Vienna copycat start, but this time his own queen comes out to play the hero. }
4... Qf6 { His queen guards g7 from f6 and glares down the column at f2. It looks as if f2 needs a guard. Does it, or is she the one in trouble? [%cal Rf6g7,Rf6f2] } 5. Nd5 { Forget f2: the knight jumps in and hits her on f6 and the pawn on c7 at the same time, so she has no time for anything else. [%cal Gd5f6,Gd5c7] } (5. Qxg7 { Wait before grabbing: his queen sits right there on f6, looking straight back at you. } 5... Qxg7 { She simply recaptures. You have just handed over your whole queen for a single pawn. [%cal Rf6g7] }) (5. Qg3 { A sensible retreat that keeps the game roughly level. } 5... Nge7 6. Nf3 Nd4 { Nothing wrong here, but you missed a far sharper answer on the board. }) (5. Qf3 { Offering a trade, though his queen was already the badly placed one. } 5... Nd4 6. Qxf6 Nxf6 { Trading queens here hands away your best attacking chance, and he is perfectly comfortable now. }) (5. d4 { Opening lines in the centre while his queen still sits on f6, exposed. } 5... d6 6. Qg3 Nxd4 { His knight grabs the pawn, and you stand worse for having let the moment pass. }) (5. Nh3 { The obvious guard for f2, and it walks into the d pawn trick from the last chapter. [%cal Gh3f2] } 5... d5 { His pawn hits the bishop on c4 and opens the bishop on c8 against your queen. [%cal Rd5c4,Rc8g4] } 6. Qg3 dxc4 { She ran and the bishop fell: a whole piece gone, for guarding a pawn that did not need it. }) (5. Nf3 { Development, but it does nothing for f2 and nothing about the d pawn. } 5... d5 { The push hits your bishop and uncovers his bishop on your queen at once. [%cal Rd5c4,Rc8g4] } 6. Qg3 dxc4 { A whole bishop lost to one pawn move. }) 5... Qxf2+ { She grabs the pawn with check. Let her: from here on she is the one running. } 6. Kd1 { The only legal move on the whole board. Step aside and smile. [%csl Gd1] } 6... Kf8 { He tucks the king away to hold g7; g6 would meet the same hunt, and the knight to f6 would lose g7 and the rook behind it. Now look at his queen on f2, deep in your camp and all alone. Which piece can go after her? } 7. Nh3 { The knight attacks her on f2, and she cannot go back the way she came: the knight on d5 watches f4 and f6. If she runs to d4, the pawn to d3 frees your bishop and you are clearly better. [%cal Gh3f2,Gd5f4,Gd5f6] } (7. Qxg7+ { Look again before grabbing: his king stands right beside that pawn. } 7... Kxg7 { The king simply eats the queen. That trade ends the game in his favour. [%cal Rf8g7] }) (7. Qf3 { Offering the trade lets her escape the whole net for free. } 7... Qxf3+ 8. Nxf3 Bd6 { Queens leave the board, and the pressure you spent three moves building has vanished with them. }) (7. Qe2 { The very same problem: you invite her to trade herself off safely. } 7... Qxe2+ 8. Nxe2 Bd6 { Material stays level, the queen walked out through the front door, and he is the one better placed now. }) (7. Nxc7 { A fork on his rook, but you let the queen on f2 go free just to grab it. } 7... d5 8. Qf3 dxc4 { His pawn scoops up your bishop on c4. Your knight does attack the rook on a8, but right now you are the one down material. }) 7... h5 { He hits your queen with the h pawn. He does not grab g2, because your queen looks straight down that column. Careful: taking his queen is not the answer either. [%cal Gg4g2] } 8. Qg5 { Out of the pawn's reach, still eyeing g7, and still covering g2. His queen has one safe square left, d4; he has an extra pawn, no castling, and nothing else out. [%cal Gg5g7,Gg5g2][%csl Gd4] } (8. Nxf2 { Taking her looks like the point of everything, but look at your own queen on g4 first. } 8... hxg4 { His h pawn takes her. Queen for queen. } 9. Nxg4 { You win the pawn back, and all the fire has gone out: level material and no attack. [%csl Rg4] }) (8. Qf3 { Offering a trade lets her walk out through the front door. } 8... Qxf3+ 9. gxf3 Bd6 { Queens off, your king side pawns are a wreck, and he keeps the extra pawn. He is better. }) (8. Qg3 { Same problem: she trades herself off at once. } 8... Qxg3 9. hxg3 Nf6 { Queens off and he is a clean pawn up with nothing to fear. }) (8. Qe2 { She steps back to e2, but he trades anyway. } 8... Qxe2+ 9. Bxe2 Bd6 { Queens gone, and his extra pawn is the only thing left to talk about. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the gambit accepted"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/X3YXFT9u"]
[Role "teaching"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_2"]

{ His other main defence brings the king knight out first. This is where the real Vienna gambit lives. }
2... Nf6 { His knight attacks your pawn on e4 and defends e5 at the same time. Throw the f pawn at him anyway. [%cal Yf2f4] } 3. f4 { The Vienna gambit: give up the f pawn to rip the position open before he is ready for it. Sound or not, across the board it plays like a monster. [%cal Gf4e5] } (3. Nf3 { The calm road, and a very solid choice in its own right. } 3... Nc6 4. d4 Bb4 { Solid, sound chess for both sides — just not the reason we picked the Vienna today. }) (3. Bc4 { Developing the bishop first, heading straight for f7, is perfectly good too. } 3... Bc5 4. Nge2 O-O { A quiet game either way. The gambit is simply the sharper weapon we want. }) (3. g3 { The quiet Vienna, with a fianchetto still to come. } 3... Bc5 4. Bg2 O-O { Playable and pleasant, but today we came for the fireworks instead. }) (3. d4 { Straight down the middle, but he simply captures it. } 3... exd4 4. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight attacks your queen on d4, and the tempo you spent is handed straight back. }) 3... exf4 { He takes the bait. Now hit the knight before it can settle anywhere. [%cal Ye4e5] } 4. e5 { The whole point of the gambit. Every square his knight can run to is bad or useless. [%cal Ge5f6] } (4. Bc4 { Developing instead of pushing lets him consolidate at once. } 4... c6 5. d4 d5 { He keeps the pawn and builds a wall behind it. Your gambit has bought nothing at all. }) (4. Nf3 { Also natural, and also a move too slow for this position. } 4... d5 5. exd5 Nxd5 { He keeps the extra pawn and still gets a perfectly fine game. }) (4. d4 { A big centre, but you left his knight completely unbothered. } 4... Bb4 5. Bd3 d5 { The pin arrives, and the chance to play e5 yourself has gone for good. }) 4... Ng8 { All the way home: he owns one cold, extra pawn, and you own the whole board. Had his queen pinned your pawn from e7 instead, your queen to e2 would break the pin and the knight would still have to run. [%csl Rg8][%cal Yg1f3] } 5. Nf3 { Develop with a free tempo while his pieces still sleep at home. [%csl Gf3] } (5. Bc4 { The bishop belongs on this square too, but the knight gets there faster. } 5... d6 6. Qe2 dxe5 { He hands the pawn back and untangles. Less than you had before. }) (5. Qe2 { The queen props up the pawn, playable, though she blocks in your own bishop. } 5... Nc6 6. Nf3 g5 { He holds onto the extra pawn, and you are the one pushing uphill now. }) (5. Qf3 { Also playable, and again the queen takes the square your knight wanted. } 5... Nc6 6. Qxf4 d6 { Material comes back level. Developing the knight to f3 first keeps far more pressure on the board. }) 5... d6 { He strikes back at your proud pawn on e5; the knight to c6 or the pawn to d5 would get the same answer. Build the full centre. [%cal Yd2d4] } 6. d4 { Two big pawns and two knights out, while his pieces are all still at home. [%csl Ge5,Gd4] } (6. Qe2 { Also good, holding the pawn one move longer. } 6... dxe5 7. d4 Be7 { Playable, and the queen can even scoop e5 back later — building with the pawn to d4 simply does the same job with more menace. }) (6. exd6 { Releasing the tension yourself only frees up his game. } 6... Bxd6 7. d4 Ne7 { He develops with a free tempo, and the space you had has gone back to him. }) (6. Bc4 { The bishop again, though the centre was really the bigger prize here. } 6... Nc6 7. Qe2 dxe5 { He unwinds fully, and your grip on the position has loosened to dead level. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the gambit main line"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/wXXaa63l"]
[Role "exercise"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_3"]

{ The gambit sits on the board, and now he must find the sharpest answer. So must you. }
3... d5 { His strongest answer: he hits back in the centre at once. Two of your pawns can capture. Which one? } 4. fxe5 { The f pawn captures toward the centre, kicking away the knight's support on e5. [%csl Ge5] } (4. exd5 { The other capture looks natural, and it is the wrong one here. } 4... Nxd5 5. fxe5 Nxc3 6. bxc3 Qh4+ { Her check lands, and defending it costs you the right to castle. }) (4. Nf3 { Developing while leaving both central pawns hanging is too slow. } 4... exf4 5. exd5 Nxd5 { He ends up a clean pawn up with a perfectly free game. }) (4. d3 { Propping the e pawn, but the real fight is in the middle of the board. } 4... d4 5. Nce2 exf4 { His pawn claims the square your knight wanted, and you stand worse. }) (4. d4 { A big centre, but you still owed the position a capture first. } 4... exd4 5. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight attacks your queen on d4, and you stand simply worse. }) 4... Nxe4 { He grabs the pawn straight back. Now hit the knight and eye f7 at the same time. } 5. Qf3 { The queen steps out to f3, attacking the knight on e4 and squinting straight at f7. [%cal Gf3f7,Gf3e4] } (5. Nf3 { The classical road, and every bit as good. } 5... Bc5 6. d4 Bb4 { Also perfectly fine. The queen move is simply the sharper choice today. }) (5. Nxe4 { Trading off your best knight for his own. } 5... dxe4 6. d4 exd3 { That knight on e4 was worth keeping him occupied with instead of trading. }) (5. d3 { Kicking the knight with a pawn, but look at your own pawns afterward. } 5... Nxc3 6. bxc3 d4 { Your pawns end up wrecked before a single piece has even come out. }) 5... Nxc3 { He swaps knights on c3 at once, his most common answer. Your b pawn and your d pawn can both take back: which recapture helps your pieces? } 6. dxc3 { The d pawn takes back and opens the diagonal for your bishop on c1, which heads for f4 to guard your pawn on e5. [%cal Gc1f4,Gf4e5] } (6. bxc3 { The b pawn takes back, and that is just as good. } 6... c5 7. Bb5+ Nc6 { His c pawn grabs space and the check is blocked. Level: a different game, not a worse one. }) (6. Qxc3 { The queen takes back, and walks into his d pawn. } 6... d4 7. Qg3 Qd5 { His pawn chases your queen, and his own queen takes the centre. He is better. [%csl Rg3] }) 6... Be6 { His bishop guards d5, his most common move. Whether he plays this, the bishop to e7 or the knight to c6, your answer is the same, and a check on h4 is simply blocked with g3. [%cal Re6d5] } 7. Bf4 { The bishop comes to f4 and guards e5, the pawn that cramps his whole king side. [%cal Gf4e5] } (7. Qg3 { The queen to g3 is also good. } 7... d4 8. Bd2 dxc3 9. Bxc3 { The pawns come off and your bishop lands on c3 instead. Level. [%cal Gc3g7] }) (7. Ne2 { Developing the knight first is fine, just slower. } 7... Nd7 8. Bf4 c5 { You reach the bishop move a tempo later. Level. }) (7. Be3 { On e3 the bishop leaves e5 without a guard, and his queen checks. [%csl Re5] } 7... Qh4+ 8. Bf2 Qa4 { His queen gets out with tempo and eyes your queen side. About level. }) (7. Bd3 { The bishop to d3 is natural, but it lets his knight come to c6. } 7... Nc6 8. Qg3 d4 { His knight and d pawn press, and his pieces are the livelier ones. Level. }) 7... c6 { He props up d5 with the c pawn, and the knight to d7, c5 or the queen to d7 would all meet the same plan. Where does your king belong? } 8. O-O-O { Castle long: your king is safe on c1, your rook on d1 stares down the column at d5, and your bishop on f4 holds e5. That is the gambit main line, set up. [%cal Gd1d5,Gf4e5] } (8. Ne2 { The knight first is just as good. } 8... g5 9. Bd2 Nd7 { His pawn chases your bishop off f4 and his knight comes for e5. Level. }) (8. Qg3 { The queen on g3 is fine too. } 8... h6 9. h4 Nd7 { His knight heads for e5 and c5. Level. }) (8. Bd3 { The bishop to d3 lets his g pawn chase yours from f4. } 8... g5 9. Be3 Nd7 { Your bishop is kicked back and his knight hits e5. Level. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the gambit, pin the knight"]
