[Role "exercise"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/8/3pP3/4n3/2N2Q2/PPPP2PP/R1B1KBNR b KQkq - 1 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/8/3pP3/4n3/2N2Q2/PPPP2PP/R1B1KBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_5"]

{ The gambit main line: your queen on f3 attacks his knight on e4 and eyes f7. He has more than one way to meet it. }
5... Nc6 { He develops the knight to c6, and now taking on e4 at once runs into a jump to d4 at your queen. Can you still win the knight on e4? } 6. Bb5 { The bishop pins the knight on c6 against his king, while your queen keeps hitting the other one on e4. [%cal Gb5c6,Gf3e4] } (6. Nxe4 { Taking the knight now only lets him off the hook. } 6... Nd4 7. Qc3 dxe4 { His knight lands in the middle with tempo, and you stand worse. }) (6. Qf4 { Defending nothing important and stepping straight into his pawns. } 6... g5 7. Qf3 Nb4 { His pawn kicks the queen and his knight jumps in with a fork on c2 coming. You are in real trouble here. }) (6. Qe2 { Defending the pin square, but all the pressure has drained away. } 6... Bf5 7. Nf3 Bc5 { He has developed every piece while you have developed none. }) 6... Nxc3 { He trades knights on c3. Two pawns can take back. Which one? } 7. dxc3 { The d pawn takes back, and the bishop on c1 sees the whole diagonal to h6. The b pawn stays home, so your queen side has no holes, and the queen still leans on f7. [%cal Gc1h6,Gf3f7] } (7. bxc3 { The b pawn takes back, and it opens nothing: the bishop on c1 is still shut in behind d2, and your a pawn now stands alone. } 7... Qh4+ 8. Qf2 Qxf2+ { The queens come off, and with your a pawn alone and the bishop still boxed in, he stands a shade better. }) (7. Qxc3 { The queen recaptures, and now she becomes the one getting chased. } 7... Qh4+ 8. Qg3 Qxg3+ { She takes your queen with check again — recapture and the queens leave the board while your king still sits in the centre. }) (7. Bxc6+ { A capture first, though it only helps repair his pawns. } 7... bxc6 8. dxc3 Qh4+ { You have strengthened his central pawns for absolutely nothing. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the gambit, kick the knight"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/RZNvG1D2"]
[Role "exercise"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/8/3pP3/4n3/2N2Q2/PPPP2PP/R1B1KBNR b KQkq - 1 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/8/3pP3/4n3/2N2Q2/PPPP2PP/R1B1KBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_5"]

{ Your queen on f3 hits his knight on e4, and he must decide how to hold it. }
5... f5 { He props the knight up with the f pawn, and now it cannot simply be taken. How do you shift it? } 6. d3 { The d pawn kicks the knight, and it has nothing better than a trade on c3. [%cal Gd3e4] } (6. d4 { The d pawn two squares is playable, but it lets his bishop pin your knight. } 6... Bb4 7. a3 Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 { He takes on c3 and your pawns are doubled. Slightly better for him. [%csl Rc3] }) (6. exf6 { Taking his f pawn in passing lets his knight come back to f6 with a free game. } 6... Nxf6 7. d4 Bb4 { His pieces come out with gain of time. Slightly better for him. }) (6. Nxe4 { Taking the knight lets his d pawn take back with a hit on your queen. } 6... dxe4 7. Qc3 Nc6 { His pawn on e4 chases your queen and his pieces flood out. He is clearly better. }) (6. Nh3 { Heading for f4 with the knight is too slow. } 6... Nc6 7. d3 Nxc3 8. bxc3 d4 { The same kind of position as the main line, with his pieces a move ahead. He is better. }) (6. Bc4 { The bishop hits d5, but his pawn simply takes it. } 6... dxc4 7. Nxe4 fxe4 8. Qxe4 { You won a pawn back, not the bishop. A piece down. }) 6... Nxc3 { He trades on c3. Only one pawn can take back. } 7. bxc3 { The b pawn takes back, the only way, and your pawns on c3 and d3 now hold the middle of the board. } 7... d4 { His pawn goes to d4 and attacks c3, and taking it would open lines for his queen. What else can your queen do? } 8. Qg3 { The queen goes to g3: she guards e5 and blocks the check from h4. [%cal Gg3e5] } (8. Be2 { The bishop first is just as good. } 8... Nc6 9. Qg3 Be6 { The queen reaches g3 a move later, and it is the same kind of position. Level. }) (8. g3 { The g pawn blocks the check from h4, a sound idea. } 8... Nc6 9. Bg2 dxc3 { He takes on c3 while your bishop settles on g2. You are a pawn down for some play: slightly worse. }) (8. cxd4 { Taking the pawn opens the centre for his queen. } 8... Qxd4 9. Rb1 Nc6 { His queen dominates the board from d4. He is better. }) (8. Bb2 { The bishop to b2 guards c3, but slowly. [%cal Gb2c3] } 8... Nc6 9. cxd4 Nxd4 { His knight lands on d4 with a hit on your queen. Slightly better for him. }) 8... Nc6 { He develops the knight and leaves c3 hanging for later. Only one move keeps your position together here, and it would be the answer to an immediate capture on c3 as well. } 9. Be2 { The bishop comes to e2, aiming at h5: if he grabs c3 now, the check from h5 punishes him. [%cal Ge2h5] } (9. Nf3 { Developing the knight lets him win your pawn on c3. } 9... dxc3 10. Be2 Be6 { He is a pawn up, and your own knight on f3 now blocks the bishop's road to h5. He is better. }) (9. cxd4 { Taking on d4 opens the d column for his queen. } 9... Qxd4 10. Rb1 Qa4 { His queen raids your queen side. You are clearly worse. }) (9. Bd2 { The bishop to d2 guards c3, but he takes it anyway. [%cal Gd2c3] } 9... dxc3 10. Bxc3 Be6 { The pawns come off, but his pieces are faster. He is better. }) (9. Qxg7 { Grabbing the pawn on g7 walks into his bishop. } 9... Bxg7 { His bishop on f8 was guarding g7. A queen for a pawn. [%cal Rf8g7] }) 9... Be6 { His bishop comes to e6. His pawn on d4 still points at c3: how do you close the door on it? Had his queen come to d5 instead, the bishop to f3 would have hit her. } 10. c4 { The pawn to c4 locks the centre: his pawn on d4 can never take on c3 now, your pawn on e5 cramps him, and your bishop can still come to f3. [%csl Gc4,Gd3] } (10. Bf3 { The bishop to f3 is the popular choice, and it is fine. } 10... Qd7 11. c4 Nb4 { You lock the centre a move later and his knight finds b4. About level. }) (10. Nf3 { The knight lets him take on c3 at last. } 10... dxc3 11. O-O Qd7 { He is a pawn up and all his pieces are out. He is better. }) (10. Bh5+ { The check is premature now. } 10... g6 11. Bf3 Qd7 { His pawn blocks it, and your bishop reaches f3 a move later. Slightly better for him. }) (10. cxd4 { Grabbing the pawn at last lets his queen into the centre. } 10... Qxd4 11. Rb1 Qc3+ { His queen checks and raids. You are losing. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the gambit declined"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/zBVS1QMJ"]
[Role "exercise"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_3"]

{ Some players simply refuse the gambit outright. That caution hands you a pleasant game for free. }
3... d6 { He props e5 and declines the pawn. Do not grab it back yourself — what does this position actually want? } 4. Nf3 { Develop instead. The tension on e5 is yours to resolve whenever it suits you. [%csl Gf3] } (4. fxe5 { Capturing at once throws away the very tension that was working in your favour. } 4... dxe5 5. Bb5+ c6 { You released the tension for him, his pieces come alive with the check, and you stand worse. }) (4. Bb5+ { A check that only helps him improve his own pawn structure. } 4... c6 5. Be2 Nbd7 { The bishop has to retreat anyway, and he sits perfectly comfortable. }) (4. d4 { A big centre, but his pawn simply takes it and your queen comes out early. } 4... exd4 5. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight gains a tempo on your queen. Playable, but a little loose. }) (4. Be2 { Solid, quiet, and a shade slow for this position. } 4... d5 5. fxe5 Nxe4 { He strikes back in the centre before your own pieces are ready for it. }) 4... Nc6 { He brings the knight to c6, his most common move. Against the pin on g4 you would carry on just the same, and if he takes on f4 or goes knight to d7, the pawn to d4 comes first. Now finish bringing your pieces out. } 5. Be2 { Modest and correct: castle quickly, then decide later what the f pawn should do. [%csl Ge2] } (5. Bb5 { The pin is the most popular move, and just as good. } 5... exf4 6. d4 a6 7. Be2 { He takes on f4 and puts the question to your bishop; you get a big centre for the pawn. Slightly better for you. }) (5. d4 { Striking in the centre at once is also good. } 5... exd4 6. Nxd4 g6 { The centre opens and you keep a small edge. }) (5. Bc4 { The usual Vienna square, except here it runs straight into a fork trick. } 5... Nxe4 6. Nxe4 d5 { His pawn forks both your bishop and your knight at once; retreat one, recapture with the other, and the material settles back level. [%cal Rd5c4,Rd5e4] }) (5. fxe5 { Taking on e5 now releases the tension a move too early. } 5... dxe5 6. Bb5 Bc5 { Level material, and the free hand you had is gone. }) 5... Be7 { He develops the bishop, ready to castle. Had he pinned on g4 instead, the answer would be the same. Tuck your own king away first. } 6. O-O { Castled, extra space, and the f pawn ready to open the column the moment you choose. [%cal Gf1f8] } (6. d3 { Solid too, and castling can follow. } 6... O-O 7. O-O Rb8 { You castle a move later. A pleasant game for you. }) (6. d4 { Grabbing the centre while your king still sits in the middle. } 6... Nxd4 7. Nxd4 exd4 8. Qxd4 { You win the pawn back, but your queen is out early and your king is still in the middle. Only a small edge. }) (6. fxe5 { Taking on e5 still helps him more than you. } 6... dxe5 7. Bb5 Bd7 { Level material. The half-open f column was worth more than that trade. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: he ignores the gambit"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/q2gn8vjV"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_3"]
[Role "exercise"]

{He ignores your gambit pawn on f4 and brings out a second knight instead. That lets you open the centre with a capture that also wins a tempo.}
3... Nc6 {His knight develops to c6, leaving the pawn on f4 completely alone. Capture on e5 now and make him spend a move winning it back.} 4. fxe5 {The capture is strong now: his knight on c6 has to leave its post to recover the pawn, so you come out a tempo ahead. [%csl Ge5]} (4. Nf3 {Quiet development lets him take back on f4 at his leisure, so the pawn you offered never really costs him anything.} 4... exf4 5. Bc4 Bb4 {He recaptures on f4 whenever it suits him, and you have nothing to show for the pawn you gave up.}) (4. Bb5 {The pin looks useful, but the pawn on e5 was there to be won first.} 4... exf4 5. Nf3 d5 {He grabs the pawn back and strikes in the centre with his own pawn to d5. The position is level at best.}) (4. d3 {Propping the pawn with a pawn move wastes the moment — the pawn on e5 was yours for the taking.} 4... Bb4 5. fxe5 Nxe5 { He settles in comfortably: by the time you take on e5 his knight is ready to take straight back. }) (4. Nd5 {The knight jumps to d5, defended by the pawn on e4, but it also leaves that same pawn without any other guard.} 4... Nxe4 5. Nf3 Nf6 {He grabs the loose pawn on e4 instead, and you are left both a pawn down and behind in development.}) 4... Nxe5 {His knight recaptures and sits in the middle of the board on e5. Push the pawn to d4 and kick it straight back.} 5. d4 {The pawn on d4 attacks the knight directly, and there is no square left where it stands comfortably. [%cal Gd4e5]} (5. Nf3 {This offers a trade rather than a second tempo — his knight is only too happy to come off on f3.} 5... Nxf3+ 6. Qxf3 d5 {He trades gladly and the position levels out completely. The direct pawn push kept far more pressure on.}) (5. Nge2 {Quiet development leaves his knight sitting comfortably in the centre on e5 for now.} 5... c5 6. d4 cxd4 {He gets time to prop the knight up with his own pawn to c5, and half of your advantage has gone with it.}) (5. Be2 {A modest developing move, but it hands him a free move to strike back in the centre himself.} 5... d5 6. d4 Ng6 {He gets his own pawn to d5 for free, and the position is close to level.}) (5. Bb5 {It looks like a pin toward your king, but his own pawn on d7 blocks the diagonal completely — the bishop is doing nothing at all.} 5... Ng6 6. d4 Bb4 {He gets his knight to safety and his bishop to an active square, and you have spent a tempo for nothing.}) 5... Ng6 {The knight retreats to the rim on g6; back to c6 would change nothing. Push the pawn to e5 and kick the other knight, still sitting on f6, straight home.} 6. e5 {The pawn advances again, and this time it is his king's knight that has nowhere good to run. [%cal Ge5f6]} (6. Nf3 {Solid development, but it skips the second free kick that the pawn push to e5 was offering.} 6... d5 7. e5 Ne4 {He gets his knight to the strong square on e4 a move earlier than he should, because you let him strike in the centre first.}) (6. Bd3 {The bishop eyes the knight on g6, a fair idea, just slower than simply pushing the pawn.} 6... d5 7. e5 Ne4 {His knight settles on the strong e4 square all the same — exactly what the immediate pawn push to e5 was meant to prevent.}) (6. Qd3 {The queen comes out before your e pawn has finished chasing his last knight home.} 6... Bb4 7. Ne2 O-O {He pins the knight on c3 and then castles into safety. Your edge has shrunk to almost nothing.}) (6. Bc4 {The bishop heads for its favourite diagonal, but it is one move early — the pawn push to e5 was the more urgent job.} 6... c6 7. e5 d5 {You still get the pawn to e5 in, but his pawns on c6 and d5 close the centre and your attack slows right down.}) 6... Ng8 {Both his knights have been kicked backwards, one to g6 and the other all the way home. No tactic here: just a plain developing move that keeps both centre pawns guarded.} 7. Nf3 {Two big pawns stand in the centre, on d4 and e5, and both of his knights are still tangled up behind them. That lead is the whole reward for ignoring his gambit pawn. [%csl Ge5,Gd4]} (7. h4 {The rook pawn storms forward. It is a sharp try, just a wilder road than simply finishing development first.} 7... d5 8. h5 N6e7 {The knight retreats again and the position stays just as pleasant for you — developing first would have been the calmer road to the same place.}) (7. Bd3 {A fine square for the bishop, pointing straight at h7.} 7... d6 8. Qe2 dxe5 {The position stays comfortable either way — bringing the knight to f3 first simply keeps your initiative rolling a touch faster.}) (7. Qe2 {The queen adds support to the centre pawns. Nothing wrong with it, just less urgent than bringing the last knight out.} 7... Bb4 8. Bd2 Bxc3 { The trade is close to even, and it shows that the knight belonged on f3 all along. }) (7. Bc4 { The bishop comes out, but the pawn on e5 has lost its guard. } 7... Nxe5 8. Bb3 Ng6 { His knight eats the pawn on e5, and half your big centre is gone. Only a small edge left. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the early bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/mk3IpsGO"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_2"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ He brings the bishop out before either knight. Different door, same house: your Vienna set-up does not change, but one trick along the way does. }
2... Bc5 { The bishop comes out early and stares at f2. Nothing changes for you: where does your own bishop belong in the Vienna? } 3. Bc4 { Bishop for bishop: yours stares at f7 just as his stares at f2, and the plan is the one you know — d3, then the f pawn. [%cal Gc4f7] } (3. Nf3 { A fine move that wins its share of games, but the knight sits down in front of the f pawn. That is the Italian, not the Vienna. } 3... d6 4. d4 exd4 5. Nxd4 { A good open game for you, and not the set-up you are here to learn. }) (3. Qh5 { The queen raid, hitting e5 and f7 at once. } 3... d6 4. Bc4 Qf6 { He guards everything with a pawn and his queen, and yours is now a target. Playable, but the raid bought nothing. }) (3. f4 { The Vienna push a move too soon: with nothing on d3 or c4 yet, his bishop on c5 stares at the hole on f2, and he simply props e5 with a pawn. } 3... d6 4. Nf3 Bg4 { Level. The pin on f3 is annoying and your king has no safe home yet. Bishop out and d3 in first, then push. }) (3. Na4 { Chasing the bishop from the rim looks clever, and it walks into a sacrifice. } 3... Bxf2+ { He takes on f2 with check. [%cal Rc5f2] } 4. Kxf2 Qh4+ 5. Ke3 Qf4+ { The check on h4 dragged your king out, and now it is out in the middle of the board on move five. You are worse. [%csl Re3] }) (3. g3 { The quiet fianchetto is sound, but slow. } 3... a5 4. Bg2 Nc6 { Level, and the f pawn storm is a long way off. }) 3... Nf6 { His knight comes out and eyes your pawn on e4; d6 first would come to the same thing after your pawn to d3. Same answer as always. } 4. d3 { The little pawn move guards e4 and keeps the f pawn free: the Vienna you know. After h6 you push as usual, but after c6 or castling bring the knight to f3 first, because f4 at once runs into d5. [%csl Gd3][%cal Gd3e4,Gf2f4] } (4. Nge2 { Just as good: the knight comes out without blocking the f pawn. Playing d3 first simply keeps e4 guarded before the knight leaves g1. } 4... Nc6 5. O-O Na5 { His knight goes hunting your bishop, the same chase as in the knight-hunt chapter. Level either way. }) (4. Nf3 { Natural, and it puts the knight right in front of the f pawn. } 4... d6 5. d4 exd4 6. Nxd4 { A perfectly good game, but it is not the Vienna: the storm never comes. }) (4. f4 { The charge, one move too early: only the knight guards e4, and he hits back in the centre. [%cal Gc3e4] } 4... d5 5. exd5 e4 { His pawn on e4 shuts your centre and takes f3 away from your knight. You are worse, and the set-up is in pieces. [%csl Re4] }) (4. Bxf7+ { Never give a whole bishop for one check. } 4... Kxf7 { He takes back and walks his king home later. A bishop for a pawn. [%cal Re8f7] }) 4... d6 { He props up e5 with the pawn, and he does not take on e4: your d pawn would just take the knight back. Now, which pawn does the Vienna throw forward? } 5. f4 { The f pawn strikes at e5, with the knight on c3 guarding e4 behind it. The Vienna set-up is on the board again, by a different door. [%cal Gf4e5,Gc3e4] } (5. Na4 { Hunting the bishop is also good, but it is not the plan. } 5... Bb4+ 6. c3 d5 { He checks, you block, he strikes in the centre. Level and messy, and the f pawn is still at home. }) (5. Nf3 { Solid, but the knight blocks the f pawn: the whole point of the Vienna is gone. } 5... c6 6. O-O O-O { A quiet Italian-style game. Fine chess, and not what you came for. [%csl Rf3] }) (5. h3 { Stopping the knight jump to g4 is a fair idea, but it is a slow one. } 5... O-O 6. Nf3 c6 { Solid and quiet, and the f pawn is still waiting behind the knight. }) (5. Bg5 { The pin bites nothing while his king is still at home. } 5... c6 6. Nf3 Nbd7 { He unpins in comfort and you have spent a move on a bishop that will be kicked. He is a shade better. }) (5. Nge2 { The knight comes out without blocking the f pawn, but look where his knight can jump now. } 5... Ng4 { The knight on g4 hits f2, and only your king guards it. [%cal Rg4f2] } 6. d4 exd4 { You are already worse: your centre is falling apart while the knight on g4 hangs over f2. }) 5... Ng4 { His knight jumps to g4, eyeing a fork on f2. It looks like you must defend. Before you do, ask what is actually holding that knight on g4. [%cal Rg4f2] } 6. f5 { The pawn steps to f5 and shuts his bishop on c8 out of g4, so your queen stares at a knight nothing defends. He has to do something about it. [%cal Gd1g4][%csl Gf5] } (6. Nh3 { The obvious guard for f2, and it loses on the spot. [%cal Gh3f2] } 6... Qh4+ { Check from h4, down the diagonal your f pawn left open. [%cal Rh4e1] } 7. Kd2 Nf2 { The fork lands anyway: queen and rook attacked, with your king already out on d2. [%cal Rf2d1,Rf2h1] }) (6. Nf3 { Development that guards nothing on f2: the fork lands. } 6... Nf2 7. Qe2 Nxh1 { Your queen stepped aside and the knight took the rook in the corner. A rook for a knight, gone. [%cal Rf2h1] }) (6. Qf3 { The queen guards f2, but the fork still hits your rook. [%cal Gf3f2] } 6... Nf2 7. Qh5 Be6 { Your rook on h1 is hanging, his bishop shuts your queen out of f7, and you are worse. }) (6. fxe5 { Taking on e5 lets him take on g1. } 6... Bxg1 7. Rxg1 Qh4+ { He took your knight, and now the check on h4 drags your king out with the rook stuck in the corner. [%cal Rh4e1] }) (6. Qe2 { The queen guards f2 from e2, but now the pawn on f4 hangs. [%cal Ge2f2][%csl Rf4] } 6... exf4 7. Nf3 Bf2+ { Check, and your king loses castling: his pieces are all over your king side. }) 6... Nf2 { He jumps in anyway and forks your queen and rook, as most players would. It is a mistake: the h pawn to h5, guarding the knight, was his move, and a check on h4 would have met the pawn to g3. Can you save both? [%cal Rf2d1,Rf2h1] } 7. Qh5 { Save the queen with a threat: from h5 she hits f7 together with your bishop on c4, and only his king guards it. Mate on f7 is threatened, so he has no time for your rook. [%cal Gh5f7,Gc4f7] } (7. Qf3 { The queen escapes to f3 and even attacks the knight, but his bishop on c5 guards it, and your rook on h1 still hangs. [%cal Rc5f2] } 7... c6 8. Qh5 Qe7 { You reach h5 a move late: his queen now guards f7, his c pawn has taken d5 from your knight, and the rook on h1 is still there for the taking. He is better. [%cal Re7f7][%csl Rh1] }) (7. Qe2 { The queen steps out of the fork, and the rook on h1 is left to its fate. } 7... b5 8. Nxb5 Nxh1 { He gives a pawn to pull your knight away from d5, then takes the rook in the corner. His knight may get stuck on h1, but he is the one who is better. [%csl Rh1] }) (7. Qd2 { The queen runs to d2 and leaves the rook behind. } 7... Nxh1 8. Nf3 Nc6 { He takes the rook and calmly develops. His knight on h1 may be trapped one day, but a whole rook is a lot to pay for that hope. [%csl Rh1] }) (7. Nf3 { Development, and it leaves your queen right where the fork hits her. } 7... Nxd1 8. Kxd1 { The knight takes your queen and your king takes the knight: a queen for a knight. [%csl Rd1] }) (7. Bxf7+ { A check on f7, but your queen is not there to help yet. } 7... Kxf7 8. Qh5+ g6 { His king took the bishop and the pawn on g6 blocks the check. A bishop down, and the knight still forks your rook. [%cal Rf2h1] }) (7. Qg4 { The queen jumps to hit the pawn on g7, onto a square the knight on f2 covers. } 7... Nxg4 { The knight takes her. A whole queen for nothing. [%cal Rf2g4] }) 7... Nxh1 { He grabs the rook anyway, a mistake that loses at once: the pawn to g6, chasing your queen off, was his only defence, and castling or the queen to d7 fail too. Finish it. } 8. Qxf7# { Mate! The queen takes on f7, your bishop on c4 guards her, and every square around his king is covered. He took a rook and lost the game. [%cal Gc4f7][%csl Gf7] } (8. Bxf7+ { Check on f7, and it looks just as strong. But it is not mate. } 8... Kf8 { His king slips to f8, and now your own bishop stands in your queen's way on f7. Still good for you, but the queen could have taken on f7 with mate. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the timid d6"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/dLwM07OS"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_2"]
[Role "exercise"]

{Against the Vienna, when he refuses to fight for the centre at all, take every square of it and be glad to.}
2... d6 {A timid little move. It only guards the pawn on e5 and does nothing else at all. Seize the whole centre while you can.} 3. d4 {Straight ahead — he must now either give up the centre entirely or defend it a pawn short. [%csl Gd4]} (3. f4 {The gambit again, but here his pawn on d6 is already propping up the pawn on e5, so the sacrifice buys much less than usual.} 3... exf4 4. Qf3 Nc6 {He takes the pawn on f4 back and develops the knight to c6. You have handed him the whole centre for nothing.}) (3. Nf3 {Solid development. There is nothing wrong with it, it is only that the whole centre was there for the pawn to take.} 3... c5 4. Bc4 Be7 {A perfectly normal game. You turned down the free central space that d4 would have grabbed outright.}) (3. Bc4 {The bishop comes out first. Also playable, just a little less to the point than striking in the centre.} 3... Be7 4. Nf3 c5 {Roughly equal chess. The pawn to d4 asks a far harder question of his position than this does.}) (3. Nge2 {A quiet route for the knight, while the centre was still begging to be taken.} 3... Nf6 4. d4 c6 {You get the pawn to d4 in anyway, just one move later than you needed to.}) (3. g3 {The fianchetto. Solid enough, but it turns down the whole centre that was on offer.} 3... Nf6 4. Bg2 g6 {He gets his pieces out for free while you fianchetto, and the moment to grab the centre has passed.}) 3... exd4 { He gives up the centre at once and takes on d4, his most common reply. If he keeps the tension with a knight to f6 or d7 instead, develop with the knight to f3 and the bishop to c4. Here, take back. } 4. Qxd4 { The queen takes back and stands in the middle of the board. He will kick her with a knight; let him. [%csl Gd4] } (4. Nf3 { Developing the knight forgets that his pawn on d4 attacks your knight on c3. } 4... dxc3 5. Bb5+ c6 { He took a whole knight, and your check changes nothing. [%csl Rc3] }) (4. Nd5 { The knight jump dodges the pawn, but your queen was the piece to take back with. } 4... c6 5. Nf4 c5 { He keeps the extra pawn on d4 and a firm grip on the centre. Slightly better for him. }) 4... Nc6 { His knight jumps out with a hit on your queen. Had he played the knight to f6 instead, the bishop to e3 and castling long would follow. Where should she go? } 5. Qe3 { The queen steps back to e3, out of his knights' reach, and guards your pawn on e4. [%cal Ge3e4] } (5. Bb5 { The pin is just as popular. } 5... Ne7 6. b3 a6 7. Bxc6+ Nxc6 { Bishop for knight, and the game is about level. }) (5. Qd3 { Just as good: on d3 the queen also stays out of the way. } 5... g6 6. Be3 Bg7 { Equally pleasant for you. }) (5. Qd1 { All the way home is safe, but passive. } 5... Nf6 6. Nge2 g6 { He develops freely. A small edge at most. }) (5. Qd2 { On d2 the queen shuts in your own bishop. } 5... Nf6 6. Bb5 Be7 { Your bishop on c1 is stuck behind her, and the edge has gone. Level. }) 5... Nf6 { He brings out the king knight, his most common move, and g6 would meet the same plan. Your king is heading for the queen side. } 6. Bd2 { The bishop to d2 clears the way for castling long. [%csl Gd2] } (6. Nf3 { The knight to f3 is natural, but it lets his knight jump to g4 at your queen. } 6... g6 7. b3 Ng4 { His knight hits your queen and f2. Only a small edge left. }) (6. Bc4 { The bishop to c4 invites a trade on e6. } 6... Be6 7. Bxe6 fxe6 { The bishops come off and his pawn on e6 guards d5. Level. [%cal Re6d5] }) (6. h3 { A waiting move that lets him strike in the centre. } 6... Be7 7. Bd2 d5 { He gets the pawn to d5 in and his pieces breathe. Level. }) 6... Be7 { He develops the bishop and gets ready to castle short; the bishop to e6 or the pawn to g6 would meet the same plan. Your king goes the other way. } 7. O-O-O { Castled long: your king is tucked away on c1, and your rook on d1 already stares at his pawn on d6, the weakness his timid move chose. [%cal Gd1d6] } (7. Nf3 { The knight first is fine too. } 7... d5 8. exd5 Nxd5 { He frees his game with d5 before you castle; you keep a small edge. }) (7. Qg3 { The queen to g3 is fine as well. } 7... O-O 8. O-O-O Re8 { You castle long a move later. A pleasant game. }) (7. Bc4 { The bishop to c4 lets his knight hop to g4. } 7... O-O 8. Nf3 Ng4 { His knight hits your queen and she has to move a second time. Only a small edge. }) (7. h3 { A slow move that lets him strike in the centre. } 7... d5 8. exd5 Nxd5 { His knight lands on d5 and the tension is gone. Level. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the fake pin"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/J7TfBHsO"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_2"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ When his bishop aims at your knight and looks like a pin, check what actually happens if the bishop takes. It may not be a real pin at all. }
2... Bb4 { The bishop appears to pin the knight on c3 to your king. But your pawn on d2 is ready to take back there, so the pin has no bite. [%csl Gd2] } 3. Nd5 { Since the pin does nothing, the knight simply jumps forward, attacking the bishop on b4 and the pawn on c7 at the same time. [%cal Gd5b4,Gd5c7] } (3. d4 {Striking in the centre with the pawn is also strong, and every bit as playable as the knight jump.} 3... d6 4. dxe5 Bxc3+ {His bishop grabs the knight with check on c3. Compared to this, the immediate jump to d5 forces a much harder decision on him straight away.}) (3. a3 {Kicking the bishop with the pawn is playable, but he simply trades it for the knight at once.} 3... Bxc3 4. dxc3 d6 {Your pawns double up on the c column, and his bishop is gone for a knight — a fair trade, not a loss.}) (3. Nf3 {Quiet development, ignoring the bishop's threat completely.} 3... Bxc3 4. dxc3 d6 {He trades on c3 and doubles your pawns for free, since nothing made him think twice about it.}) (3. Qh5 {The early queen raid, aiming at the pawns on e5 and f7 together.} 3... Bxc3 4. bxc3 Qe7 {He holds everything together, and your pawns end up doubled on the c column — though the open lines and the bishop pair make up for it comfortably.}) (3. Bc4 { The normal Vienna bishop is fine, but his fake pin was an invitation to jump. } 3... Nf6 4. a3 Ba5 { A quiet, level game. The knight jump asked him a sharper question. }) 3... Bc5 { The bishop runs to c5 instead of trading. On a5 it would meet your b pawn, and after the knight to c6 you would simply take it. His king knight is still at home: you have seen what that allows. } 4. Qg4 { The queen raid you already know: she hits g7, and his knight on g8 cannot help. [%cal Gg4g7] } (4. Nxc7+ { A fork on the king and the rook on a8 — but look hard at c7 before you jump in: something of his is watching that square. [%csl Rc7] } 4... Qxc7 { His queen was watching c7 the whole time and simply recaptures. You have given up a knight for one pawn. [%cal Rd8c7] }) (4. d4 { Striking in the centre is also strong, and he takes the pawn. } 4... Bxd4 5. Nf3 Nc6 { You are a pawn down for the moment, with a lead in development to show for it. Playable, but the queen raid costs nothing at all. }) (4. b4 { Kicking the bishop with the wing pawn is playable too. } 4... Be7 5. Nf3 Nf6 { He tucks the bishop away and develops. About equal, and your b pawn is loose out there. }) (4. c3 { Building slowly with the pawn to c3 is also fine, if a touch quieter than the raid. } 4... Nf6 5. d4 Nxd5 { He trades your good knight off, and the fake pin has cost him nothing. }) (4. Nf3 { Quiet development, and it lets him kick the knight straight back. } 4... c6 5. Nc3 Qb6 { The knight went to d5 and came home again, and his queen now eyes b2. Level at best. }) 4... Bf8 { All the way home: b4, c5, f8, three moves to end up where it started. Taking on f2 instead was no better: your king takes back and nothing follows. [%cal Rc5f8] } 5. Qg3 { Keep the queen active: from g3 she still eyes g7 and e5, and she is out of the way of your d pawn and bishop. [%cal Gg3g7,Gg3e5] } (5. Qxg7 { The pawn looks free, but it is not. } 5... Bxg7 { His bishop went home for exactly this. Queen for a pawn. [%cal Rf8g7] }) (5. Nxc7+ { The fork again, and again his queen guards c7. [%cal Rd8c7] } 5... Qxc7 { A knight for a pawn, and the raid is over. [%cal Rd8c7] }) (5. Nf3 { Also good, though he can offer a queen trade. } 5... d6 6. Qh4 Qxh4 7. Nxh4 { Queens off and his king breathes again. Keep her on the board instead. }) (5. d3 { Solid, and you play the queen to g3 a move later anyway. } 5... d6 6. Qg3 Nf6 { You reach the same kind of position a move later, with nothing lost. }) 5... d6 { He props up e5 with the pawn. One more piece out, and then count the score. } 6. Bc4 { Count the pieces out: your queen, knight and bishop are in play, and not one of his has left the back row. That is what the fake pin cost him. [%csl Gg3,Gd5,Gc4] } (6. f4 { The Vienna push works here too, and it is just as good. } 6... Nf6 7. Nxf6+ Qxf6 { A sharper game, and a fine one. The bishop move keeps every piece on the board and the lead in development plain to see. }) (6. Nf3 { Also fine. He develops with a hit on your knight and you trade it off; the bishop move keeps that knight where it is. } 6... Nf6 7. Nxf6+ Qxf6 { Level material and a pleasant game, with your best piece traded away. }) (6. Nxc7+ { The fork again, and his queen still guards c7. [%cal Rd8c7] } 6... Qxc7 { His queen takes the knight. A whole piece for a pawn. [%cal Rd8c7] }) (6. Bb5+ { A check that only helps him: his c pawn blocks it and kicks your knight in the same move. } 6... c6 7. Ba4 b5 { The bishop is chased to the rim and your knight on d5 is about to be taken. You are worse. }) 1-0
